'''Cerdanyan Beron''', commonly known as just '''Cerdanés''' ({{IPA|oc|seɾðaˈnes}}; [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] and {{langx|bl|Cerdanés}}; {{langx|ca|Cerdanés}}) is the variety of the [[Beron language]] spoken in [[Cerdanya]], [[Balisca]]. Cerdanés is one of the territorialized languages of Cerdanya, alongside [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], Baliscano, and the [[Walldungu language|Walldungu]] varieties.

The dialect is regulated by the [[Institut d'Estudis Cerdanhés]], established in 1987 as part of the confederal linguistic normalization programme.

== History ==

=== ''Rodamóns'' and the founding of Cerdanya ===

The history of Cerdanés is inseparable from the history of the [[Reapers' War]] (1578–1608) and the expulsion of the Cathars from the [[Bayara|Marañón basin]]. The Volisanian and Beron-speaking Cathars of the Marañón — descendants of settlers who had established themselves in the Estuarian wetlands and the [[Marañón basin]] during the early [[Great Disappointment|colonial rush for settlement]] — were targeted by Xacano Catholic militias in a campaign of religious violence that destroyed their settlements, enslaved their indigenous allies, and drove the survivors into flight.

The refugees — known pejoratively as ''Rodamóns'' ({{Literal translation|wanderers|vagabonds}}), a term coined by their Catholic persecutors to describe the often impoverished Catharist communities subjected to massacres and forced expulsions — undertook a perilous journey north across the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. Those who survived the crossing established themselves along the northern coast and the river valleys beyond the mountains, founding the [[Colony of Cerdanya]] as a Catharist refuge.

The Beron that the Rodamóns carried with them was already a colonial variety — shaped by decades of contact with Volisanian, Xacano, and indigenous languages in the Marañón basin — but it remained fundamentally a Beron dialect, retaining the characteristic phonological and grammatical features of the metropolitan language. The crossing of the Matarrañya, however, initiated a new phase of linguistic development:  immersed in a multilingual environment where Volisanian was the language of colonial administration and Walldungu was the language of the indigenous population, Cerdanés began to diverge from its parent dialect.

==== Colonial period (1608–1738) ====
{{Main|Commonwealth of Cerdanya}}
During the two centuries of colonial rule, Cerdanés developed in a sociolinguistic environment shaped by three forces: the dominance of Volisanian as the language of colonial government and the Cathar ecclesiastical structure; the persistence of Beron as the vernacular of the Rodamón communities and their descendants; and the interactions with the Walldungu, which contributed vocabulary and phonological features to the Cerdanyan dialect through sustained contact in the river valleys and the coastal settlements.

The colonial period saw the emergence of Cerdanés as a distinct dialect, differentiated from the Beron of the metropole by several features: the deaspiration of /h/, the reduction of feminine plural ''-AS'' to ''-ES'', the simplification of plural definite articles to ''es'', and the adoption of Volisanian and Wallche loanwords in domains where the colonial experience had created new concepts without Beron equivalents. The dialect also developed a distinctive intonation pattern, attributed by linguists to the influence of Volisanian prosody on Beron phonology.

=== Post-independence and the Baliscano ascendency (1808–1919) ===
{{Main|Baliscano|Cosmocazo|Vergonha}}
The independence of Balisca and the subsequent rise of [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] as the national lingua franca created a new pressure on Cerdanés. Baliscano — itself a creole with a substantial Volisanian and Beron lexical component — became the language of intercommunal communication, commerce, and eventually education, reducing Cerdanés to a vernacular spoken primarily in the home and the community. The 19th century saw a gradual shift toward Baliscano in the urban centres, particularly in the urban areas of [[Merced]] and [[Concepción]], while Cerdanés remained dominant in the rural valleys and smaller towns.

The period also saw the first literary production in Cerdanés: the [[Cançonèr Cerdanhés]] ("Cerdanyan Songbook"), a collection of folk poetry and oral tradition compiled by the Beron folklorist [[Pèire de la Sèrra]] between 1842 and 1856, which preserved the dialect's oral literature at a time when it was beginning to retreat from public life. Amongst these folk songs was ''[[W:Aqueras Montanhas|Aqueras Montanhas]]'' ("those mountains"), which was sung by the ''Rodamóns'' as they crossed the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén|Matarrañya]]. It would be adopted as the [[W:National anthem|national anthem]] of Cerdanya in 1902.

=== Baliscan Revolution and linguistic normalization (1919–present) ===
{{Main|Baliscan Revolution|Processo Revolucionario en Curso}}
Radical decentralization the subsequent process of linguistic normalization transformed the status of Cerdanés. The Revolution's emphasis on communal self-management and cultural pluralism created a political framework in which regional languages were no longer seen as obstacles to national unity but as expressions of the confederation's diversity. The [[1928 Confederal Language Inititiave]] recognized Beron as a territorialized language of Cerdanya, and the establishment of the [[Institut d'Estudis Cerdanhés]] in 1928 provided the dialect with its first regulatory body.

The late 20th and early 21st centuries have seen a revitalisation of Cerdanés, driven by the same forces that have stengthened Beron in Volisanian: the introduction of [[W:Bilingual education|bilingual education]], the use of the dialect in local media, and the growing cultural prestige of regional identity in a globalised world. The 2025 census recorded approximately 3 million speakers of Cerdanés — a figure that includes both native speakers and those who have acquired the dialect through education — representing roughly 6.7% of the Cerdanyan population. The dialect is strongest in the rural valleys north of the Matarrañya and in the smaller towns of the interior, while Baliscano remains dominant in the Mercepción conurbation and the coastal cities.

== Phonology ==
Cerdanés shares the core phonological features of metropolitan Beron, with several developments specific to the Cerdanyan context:

=== Consonants ===

* '''Mesogean F > H (then deaspirated)''': Inherited Mesogean initial /f/ became /h/ in Beron and was subsequently deaspirated in Cerdanés, leaving a glide or zero onset: ''huec'' /wek/ "fire" (cf. Volisanian ''foc''), ''hèr'' /hɛɾ/ "iron" (cf. Volisanian ''ferro''). The /h/ is retained only in the more isolated communities near the Matarrañya.
* '''Mesogean LL > TH''': ''vedèth'' /beˈdɛt(ʃ)/ "calf," ''eth'' /et(ʃ)/ "the" (masc. singular article), ''era'' /eˈɾa/ "the" (fem. singular article).
* '''Loss of N intervocalically''': ''lua'' "moon" (cf. Volisanian ''lluna''), ''haria'' "flour" (cf. Volisanian ''farina'').
* '''Prosthetic A- before initial R-''': ''arríder'' "to laugh," ''arreconéisher'' "to recognize."
* '''Intervocalic /b/ > [w]''': ''cantaua'' /kanˈtawa/ "was singing" (cf. standard Beron ''cantava'').

=== Vowels ===
Cerdanés retains the seven-vowel system of metropolitan Beron in stressed syllables. The dialect's vowel system has been influenced by sustained contact with Volisanian, which shares the same seven-vowel inventory in stressed position, reinforcing the stability of the system against the kind of reduction that has occurred in some other Beron varieties.

=== Specific Cerdanyan features ===
Several features distinguish Cerdanés from other Beron varieties and reflect the dialect's specific history in Balisca:

* '''Reduction of plural definite articles to ''es''''': The metropolitan Beron distinction between masculine ''eths'' and feminine ''eras'' has been levelled to ''es'' /es/ in Cerdanés.
* '''Feminine plural ''-AS'' > ''-ES''''': ''hemnes'' /ˈennes/ "women" (cf. metropolitan Beron ''hemnas''), ''parles'' /ˈpaɾeles/ "you speak."
* '''Wallche substrate influence''': Cerdanés has absorbed a number of Wallche phonological features, particularly in the intonation patterns of speakers in the northern valleys, where the dialect's prosody shows a characteristic rising-falling contour attributed to Wallche influence.
* '''Volisanian lexical calques''': In addition to direct loanwords, Cerdanés has developed calques on Volisanian patterns: ''darrièr'' "behind" (cf. Volisanian ''darrere''), ''abans'' "before" (cf. Volisanian ''abans'').

== Grammar ==
Cerdanés grammar follows the general metropolitan Beron pattern, with the following notable features:

* '''Definite articles''': ''eth'' (masc. sing. before consonant), ''er'' (masc. sing. before vowel), ''era'' (fem. sing.), ''es'' (plural, both genders). The reduction of the plural articles to a single form is a Cerdanyan innovation.
* '''Verbal system''': Cerdanés retains the Beron synthetic verbal system, with the characteristic use of periphrastic constructions for the past tense: ''èi cantat'' "I have sung," ''èri a cantat'' "I was singing."
* '''Word order''': As in Beron, Cerdanés allows both subject-verb-object and object-verb-subject orders, with the latter particularly common in narrative and emphatic contexts.

== Lexicon ==
The Cerdanés lexicon reflects the dialect's layered history:

* '''Beron substrate''': The core vocabulary of Cerdanés is metropolitan Beron — the everyday words for family, agriculture, weather, landscape, and the body that the Rodamóns carried across the Matarrañya.
* '''Volisanian loanwords''': Centuries of coexistence with Volisanian have produced a substantial layer of Volisanian loans, particularly in domains of administration, technology, and urban life: ''oficina'' "office," ''empresa'' "business," ''matrícula'' "registration," ''actuar'' "to act."
* '''Walldungu loanwords''': Contact with the [[Wallche]] has contributed vocabulary in domains of ecology, food, and material culture: ''pehuen'' "araucaria" (from Walldungu ''pewen''), ''lonko'' "chief", ''machi'' "healer", ''lof'' "settlement".
* '''Baliscano loanwords''': The rise of Baliscano as the national lingua franca has introduced a layer of creole vocabulary, particularly among younger speakers and in urban contexts: ''mercado'' "market," ''trabajo'' "work," ''sociedat'' "society."

== Orthography ==
Cerdanés is written in the [[W:Latin script|Mesogean script]], using the standard Beron orthography established by [[Loís Alibèrt]] and adapted for the Cerdanyan context by the [[Institut d'Estudis Cerdanhés]]. The orthography employs the same digraphs as standard Beron — ''nh'' for /ɲ/, ''lh'' for /ʎ/, ''th'' for /t/ or /tʃ/, ''sh'' for /ʃ/ — with the following Cerdanyan-specific conventions:

* The reduction of plural articles to ''es'' is reflected in the orthography.
* The feminine plural ending ''-es'' is written as such, distinguishing it from the metropolitan Beron ''-as''.
* The intervocalic /w/ derived from /b/ is written ''u'', following the Cerdanyan convention: ''cantaua'' rather than ''cantava''.
* Walldungu loanwords are written in their standard Walldungu orthography when used in Cerdanés text: ''pehuen'', ''lonko'', ''machi'', ''lof''.

== Sociolinguistic situation ==

Cerdanés occupies a complex position in Cerdanya's linguistic landscape. It is one of four territorialized languages in the community — alongside [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Baliscano]], and Walldungu — and is spoken by approximately 3 million people, or roughly 6.7% of the Cerdanyan population. The dialect is strongest in the rural valleys north of the Matarrañya, particularly in the Ragnilebu corridor and the smaller towns of the interior, where it functions as the primary vernacular of daily life. In the [[Mercepción]] conurbation and the coastal cities, Baliscano is dominant, though Cerdanés is widely understood and is increasingly used in cultural and political contexts as a marker of Cerdanyan identity.

The dialect's vitality has been strengthened by the confederal linguistic normalization programme, which has introduced bilingual education in Cerdanés and Baliscano, supported the production of Cerdanés-language media, and established the Institut d'Estudis Cerdanhés as the dialect's regulatory body. The 2025 census recorded that 67% of Cerdanyan residents could understand ''Cerdanés'', 42% could speak it, 38% could read it, and 22% could write it — figures that represent a significant improvement over the 1990 census, when only 28% reported being able to speak the dialect.

The relationship between Cerdanés and Volisanian is particularly significant. The two languages have coexisted in Cerdanya for over four centuries, and the degree of mutual influence is substantial. Many Cerdanyans are bilingual in Cerdanés and Volisanian, and the boundary between the two is often fluid in the communities along the Matarrañya's northern slopes, where speakers may code-switch freely between the two. The [[Institut d'Estudis Cerdanhés]] has worked to maintain the distinctiveness of Cerdanés against the pressure of Volisanian, while acknowledging that the two languages' shared history has produced a linguistic richness that is itself a form of Cerdanyan heritage.

